sd: under LOK .uno:SlideObjects reports the snap grid

Ely moves a dragged object itself: it previews the frame, snaps it
to the other objects, the slide, the guides and the grid, and drops
it with one TransformDialog. The kernel's own drag snaps the pointer
to guides, page margins, object frames and the grid within a
magnetic distance, so a client cannot show where it will land. The
grid step lives in the view (a file's settings carry it), so the
client reads it here.

gridX and gridY are the view's snap grid width, in 1/100 mm.

diff --git a/sd/source/ui/unoidl/unomodel.cxx b/sd/source/ui/unoidl/unomodel.cxx
--- a/sd/source/ui/unoidl/unomodel.cxx
+++ b/sd/source/ui/unoidl/unomodel.cxx
@@ -2429,6 +2429,9 @@
     lcl_Selection(rJson, rView);
     rJson.put("snapGrid", rView.IsGridSnap());
     rJson.put("snapGuides", rView.IsHlplSnap());
+    // The snap grid, so a client's own move snaps alike.
+    rJson.put("gridX", sal_Int64(std::lround(double(rView.GetSnapGridWidthX()))));
+    rJson.put("gridY", sal_Int64(std::lround(double(rView.GetSnapGridWidthY()))));
     auto aGuides = rJson.startArray("guides");
     const SdrHelpLineList& rLines = pPV->GetHelpLines();
     for (sal_uInt16 i = 0; i < rLines.GetCount(); ++i)
